{
  "description": "Build simple software that faithfully represents observable reality and supports human understanding and decision-making.",
  "designRules": [
    "Start from observable facts. Clearly separate observations, hypotheses, and conclusions.",
    "Prefer the simplest working solution. Remove unnecessary abstractions, decorative layers, duplicated logic, and unused code.",
    "Keep humans responsible for decisions. Software records, organizes, and presents information; it does not decide for the user.",
    "Do not present inferred intentions, emotions, or psychological states as facts.",
    "Treat observable behavior as data. Treat interpretations as hypotheses.",
    "Design for real-world constraints: light, temperature, battery, network quality, time, accessibility, and device performance.",
    "Every interface element must justify its existence. If removing an element does not reduce the user's ability to complete the current task, the element should not exist.",
    "Before implementing a feature ask: Does this help represent observable reality or support human understanding? If not, simplify or remove it."
  ],
  "processRules": [
    "Deployment starts validation. True validation happens in the field.",
    "Field observations override assumptions.",
    "Iterate from real usage; small changes are hypotheses until reality confirms them.",
    "When uncertain, ask for clarification instead of inventing assumptions."
  ],
